.TH CLRU_GET 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_get \- retrieve a value by key (type-safe convenience wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clru_get(name, key, val_ptr);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-safe GNU C statement expression that converts key to the cache's
own declared KeyT (the same way a plain C assignment would), retrieves
the stored value, and delivers it via val_ptr. The behaviour on success
depends on the value type declared at cache construction:

Non-char* val types: the stored value is converted to *val_ptr's own
type the same way a plain C assignment would (e.g. a cache storing int
values read into a double * out-param converts the int to a double,
rather than copying its raw bytes); no heap allocation occurs. val_ptr
is a pointer to *val_ptr's own type, NOT a cmap_pair *; a *val_ptr type
that cannot be implicitly converted from the cache's declared ValT is a
compile error at this point, not a silently reinterpreted value.

char* val types: ownership of the heap-allocated string is transferred
directly to the caller via *(char **)val_ptr. The caller must free()
that pointer when done.

Use clrucache_get_full() directly only if you need the raw cmap_pair
output; clru_get() hides that entirely.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name     Cache variable (must have companion type variables in scope,
         either from clru_construct / clru_declare or clru_redeclare).
         When passing a cache across function boundaries, call
         clru_redeclare() with a simple local alias first; the
         key/val-type conversion relies on the
         name##__clru_key_type_var / name##__clru_val_type_var
         companion variables.
key      Key expression; any expression implicitly convertible to KeyT.
val_ptr  Pointer to the value type, NOT cmap_pair *.
         For non-char* val types: receives the stored value converted
         to *val_ptr's own type (e.g. double * for a double out-param);
         no heap allocation occurs. Left untouched if the call does not
         return ccol_success.
         For char* val types: pointer to a char* variable; on success
         it is set to the heap-allocated string and the caller must
         free() it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success           Value delivered via val_ptr.
ccol_key_not_found     Key absent and no getter, or getter failed.
ccol_invalid_args      cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID or a stale
                       (already-destroyed) handle; or key_pair is
                       NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory Internal allocation failed.
ccol_unexpected_failure
                       A remote getter fetched a value whose size does
                       not match ValT's own size (for a non-char* val
                       type, where sizeof(ValT) is the required exact
                       size), whether this call ran the fetch itself
                       or coalesced onto another concurrent caller's
                       fetch of the same key. val_ptr is left
                       untouched.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Non-string value type: val_ptr is double *, not cmap_pair *;
   the value is copied directly into *val_ptr; no heap allocation */
clru_construct(cache, int, double, 128, NULL, NULL, NULL);

int k = 5;
double v = 2.71;
clru_set(cache, k, v);

double out = 0.0;
if (clru_get(cache, k, &out) == ccol_success) {
    printf("%.2f\en", out);
}

clru_destroy(cache);

/* String value type: caller must free the returned string */
clru_construct(str_cache, int, char *, 64, NULL, NULL, NULL);

char *greeting = "hello";
clru_set(str_cache, 1, greeting);

char *result = NULL;
if (clru_get(str_cache, 1, &result) == ccol_success) {
    printf("%s\en", result);
    free(result);
}

clru_destroy(str_cache);
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
For char* val types: the string placed in *(char **)val_ptr is a
heap-allocated copy whose ownership is transferred to the caller.
The caller MUST call free() on it when done; failing to do so is a
memory leak.  Each call to clru_get for a char* value produces an
independent allocation, so two successive calls for the same key
yield two independent pointers that must each be freed separately.

For non-char* val types: no heap allocation occurs.  The value is
converted into *val_ptr; the caller must NOT call free() on it.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}); requires GCC or Clang.
The cache never exposes a pointer into its internal storage; the value
is always copied out before the mutex is released.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_set (3),
.BR clrucache_get_full (3)
